    21	A module may declare `"selfContained": true` in its metadata. A change outside every module then stops selecting it. The declaration is the instance's statement that the module's checks read only its own directory and its declared dependencies, so it applies to `--changed-from` too. Without it, the existing safe fallback stays.
    22	
    23	## Consequences
    24	
    25	A change confined to one module reruns that module, the modules enclosing it (the root module included), the modules beneath the changed path and its declared dependents. Everything else is reused with its true origin visible. Only a changed file that no module owns widens the run to every module that does not declare itself self-contained. Consumers that compare a result's head with a subject head now see carried results as observations of the earlier head, which is accurate. An incorrect `selfContained` declaration can carry a stale result. That risk sits with the instance's explicit declaration and is documented next to it.
    26	
    27	## Alternatives considered
    28	
    29	- Hashing each check's declared inputs would be more precise. It needs every check's inputs declared and verified, and it is a larger contract.
    30	- Merging a partial `--changed-from` record into the canonical file by hand would hide which results were reused.
    31	- Carrying across matrix or engine changes would make a definition change invisible to the evidence.
